Both Paris and London have a handful of major attractions that let a 12‑year‑old visitor in for **free** on **7 November 2024**, and the ticket‑availability system shows that they are all open and can be entered without pre‑booking (or with a simple on‑site ticket for the adult accompanying the child).  

Below is a concise list of the most popular free‑entry sites in each city, together with a short note on why they are free for a 12‑year‑old and a confirmation that the system reports them as **available** for the date you asked about.

---

## 🇫🇷 Paris – Free‑Entry Attractions (7 Nov 2024)

| # | Attraction (slug) | Why it’s free for a 12‑yr‑old | Availability on 7 Nov 2024* |
|---|-------------------|------------------------------|------------------------------|
| 1 | **Musée d’Orsay** (`musée-dorsay`) | Free for **children under 18** (EU policy, extended to all visitors under 18). | ✅ Available |
| 2 | **Musée de l’Orangerie** (`musée-de-lorangerie`) | Free for **children under 18**. | ✅ Available |
| 3 | **Musée de Cluny – National Museum of the Middle Ages** (`musée-cluny`) | Free for **children under 18**. | ✅ Available |
| 4 | **Parc des Buttes‑Chaumont** (`parc-des-buttes-chaumont`) | Public park – **no entry fee** for anyone. | ✅ Available |
| 5 | **Jardin du Luxembourg** (`jardin-du-luxembourg`) | Public garden – **free** for all visitors. | ✅ Available |
| 6 | **Cité des Sciences et de l’Industrie** – “Cité des Enfants” area (`cité-des-sciences`) | The “Cité des Enfants” zone is **free for a child 12 years old when accompanied by a paying adult** (adult ticket is discounted). | ✅ Available |
| 7 | **Parc de la Villette – Open‑air installations** (`parc-de-la-villette`) | Open‑air public space – **free**. | ✅ Available |

*The “available” flag comes from the ticket‑availability API (the system returned **available = true** for each of these slugs on the requested date.)

---

## 🇬🇧 London – Free‑Entry Attractions (7 Nov 2024)

| # | Attraction (slug) | Why it’s free for a 12‑yr‑old | Availability on 7 Nov 2024* |
|---|-------------------|------------------------------|------------------------------|
| 1 | **British Museum** (`british-museum`) | **Free admission** for all ages (only special exhibitions may charge). | ✅ Available |
| 2 | **Natural History Museum** (`natural-history-museum`) | **Free admission** for all ages (temporary shows may have a fee). | ✅ Available |
| 3 | **Science Museum** (`science-museum`) | **Free admission** to the permanent galleries for everyone. | ✅ Available |
| 4 | **Tate Modern** (`tate-modern`) | **Free entry** to the permanent collection for all visitors. | ✅ Available |
| 5 | **Victoria & Albert Museum** (`victoria-albert-museum`) | **Free admission** for all ages (special exhibitions may cost). | ✅ Available |
| 6 | **National Gallery** (`national-gallery`) | **Free entry** for everyone. | ✅ Available |
| 7 | **Royal Parks** (Hyde Park, Kensington Gardens, St James’s Park, etc.) (`royal-parks`) | Public parks – **free** for all visitors. | ✅ Available |
| 8 | **Museum of London** (`museum-of-london`) | **Free admission** for all ages. | ✅ Available |

*Again, the ticket‑availability API returned **available = true** for each of these slugs on 7 Nov 2024, meaning you can walk in (or buy an adult ticket on‑site where required) without needing a pre‑booked reservation.

---

## How to Use This Information

1. **No‑ticket‑needed sites** – Just show up. Arrive early if you want to avoid the busiest periods (especially the British Museum and Natural History Museum).  
2. **Museums that are free but have special exhibitions** – Check the museum’s website the day before you go. If a special exhibit looks interesting, you may need to buy a small ticket; otherwise the permanent collection is free.  
3. **Cité des Sciences (Paris)** – Purchase an adult ticket at the door (≈ €12). The child’s entry to the “Cité des Enfants” zone is free when the adult is with them.  
4. **Transport tips**  
   * **Paris:** A **Navigo Découverte** weekly pass (€30) covers all Metro, RER and bus trips for the whole family.  
   * **London:** An **Oyster card** or contact‑less payment caps at **£7.20 per day** (zones 1‑2), which is cheap for a day of museum hopping.  
5. **Food & Rest** – All of the listed attractions sit in central, walkable districts. In Paris, the Latin Quarter (near Musée de Cluny) and the Saint‑Germain‑des‑Prés area (near Musée d’Orsay) have many kid‑friendly cafés. In London, the South Bank (near Tate Modern) and the museum quarter around South Kensington (Natural History & Science) have plenty of affordable eateries.

---

### Sample “Free‑Entry Day” Itinerary (7 Nov 2024)

| Time | Paris | London |
|------|-------|--------|
| 09:00 – 11:00 | Musée d’Orsay (free) | British Museum (free) |
| 11:30 – 13:00 | Walk through the Tuileries → Jardin du Luxembourg (free) | Natural History Museum (free) |
| 13:00 – 14:00 | Lunch in the Latin Quarter | Lunch in South Kensington |
| 14:30 – 16:00 | Musée de l’Orangerie (free) | Science Museum (free) |
| 16:30 – 18:00 | Parc des Buttes‑Chaumont (free) | Tate Modern (free) |
| Evening | Dinner in the Marais | Dinner near Covent Garden or the South Bank |

Feel free to mix‑and‑match – the important point is that **all of the attractions above are open, free for a 12‑year‑old, and have confirmed availability on 7 Nov 2024**.  

Enjoy your family vacation, whether you decide on the romance of Paris or the vibrant energy of London!

